 "method": {
  "summary": "Each O*NET task gets an AI risk and a robotics risk from task-level research data. Tasks are linked to skills through O*NET Detailed Work Activities -> Generalized Work Activities and O*NET's official skills-to-work-activities linkage. A skill's risk in an occupation is the time-share x link-relevance weighted mean of its linked tasks' risks.",
  "task_ai_risk": "Anthropic Economic Index task penetration (exact task text match) blended 0.75 / 0.25 with the Eloundou et al. GPT-4 task label (beta: E1=1, E2=0.5, E0=0). If a task has no Eloundou label the 0.25 uses the occupation prior (Eloundou occupation dv_beta, else Felten AIOE percentile, else AI composite). Tasks without Anthropic penetration use the Eloundou task label, else the prior.",
  "task_robotics_risk": "Anthropic \"What work can robots do?\" tier / 3 (E0=0, E1=0.33, E2=0.67, E3=1; non-physical rated tasks 0), raised to the tier of any cited robot system tagged to that task_id. Tasks not in the Anthropic robot file: 0.5 x (O*NET degree of automation - 1) / 4.",
  "task_combined": "1 - (1 - ai) x (1 - robotics)",
  "task_to_skill_link": "relevance(task, skill) = share of the task's DWA links whose GWA is linked to the skill in O*NET 31 essential_skills_to_work_activities / transferable_skills_to_work_activities. Weight = task time_share (Anthropic) x relevance.",
  "skill_risk": "weighted mean of linked task ai / robotics risk; combined = 1 - (1 - ai)(1 - robotics). If no task of the occupation links to the skill, the occupation's time-weighted task average is used (basis \"occupation_average\", confidence low).",
  "confidence": "high: >= 3 linked tasks and >= 70% of tasks with Anthropic task penetration; medium: >= 1 linked task; low: occupation average.",
  "bands": "safe: combined < 0.3; at_risk: combined >= 0.5; moderate otherwise. safe_skills / at_risk_skills list only skills with importance >= 2.5; the two lists are disjoint and contain skills only (never abilities or knowledge).",
  "occupation_combined": "time-weighted mean of task combined risk (derived.skill_risk_summary.occupation_combined). This is the exposure figure used by the pathway search.",
  "pathways": "Every occupation is a candidate (O*NET related gets a x1.1 bonus only). safe_overlap = sum over target skills with importance >= 2.5 of importance x coverage / total importance, where coverage = credit x level match, credit = 1 for a skill in the user's safe band, 0.5 for moderate, 0 for at_risk; level match = 1 at level >= target level - 0.5, falling linearly to 0 over the next 1.5 level points. target skills are weighted by importance x distinctiveness (generic skills every job rates highly count half; skills the target rates far above the all-occupation average count up to 1.5x); targets with fewer than 6 skills at importance >= 2.5 use their top 6. score = safe_overlap x (1 - target combined exposure) x growth_factor x gap_factor x job_zone_factor x similarity_factor x related_bonus; similarity_factor = 0.5 + 0.5 x max(0, cosine similarity of the two occupations' skill-importance profiles z-scored per skill across occupations and keeping only above-average skills, so it compares what is distinctive about each job's skill make-up; candidates below 0.3 are dropped) so the overall skill make-up counts too (safe_overlap still has the larger range: 0.3-1 vs 0.5-1); job_zone_factor = max(0.5, 1 - 0.25 x max(0, source zone - target zone - 1)) to avoid steep downshifts; growth_factor = 1 + min(growth%, 20) / 40; gap_factor = 1 - 0.5 x min(1, mean level shortfall over required target skills / 2). Eligible (future_proof) only if: target exposure <= source - 0.1 AND <= 0.85 x source; BLS 2025-35 growth >= 0 (missing growth = not eligible); safe_overlap >= 0.3; target job zone within source +2 / -1; profile similarity >= 0.3. Catch-all \", All Other\" codes are not used as targets. Top 10 kept."
 },
